[THOUGHT] The MRI request was not fulfilled — the Analyst replied “Not specified”, suggesting MRI is unavailable in this clinical environment. Per constraints, I must not repeat it or request similar advanced imaging. Instead, I should pivot to the *next most informative available test*. Given lytic bone lesions with periosteal reaction, key labs include:  
- **Serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP)**: often elevated in osteosarcoma (bone formation marker);  
- **Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)**: nonspecific but elevated in many malignancies including Ewing sarcoma;  
- **CBC**: to assess for anemia, leukocytosis (infection/inflammation), or cytopenias (marrow infiltration);  
- **ESR/CRP**: to evaluate for inflammation (e.g., osteomyelitis vs. tumor).  
Among these, ALP is most specific for osteoblastic activity and is routinely elevated in osteosarcoma — making it the highest-yield *available* lab test per standard oncology workup.

[TEST] Serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP).

[THOUGHT] The biopsy shows “small round blue cells” — a classic histopathologic description seen in several pediatric malignancies, most notably **Ewing sarcoma** and **lymphoma**, but also neuroblastoma or rhabdomyosarcoma. In the context of lytic bone lesions with periosteal reaction in an adolescent, Ewing sarcoma is the leading diagnosis. Osteosarcoma typically shows osteoid production (not small round blue cells), making it less likely. The clinical picture — trauma history (potentially coincidental), progressive pain, elevated WBC and ESR, and radiographic findings — all align. All eight evidence domains are now complete:  
1) Chief complaint & onset: left leg pain ×2 months, post-trauma;  
2) Course: progressive, unrelenting;  
3) Associated symptoms: none systemic reported, but objective inflammation (↑WBC, ↑ESR);  
4) PMH/PSH: unremarkable;  
5) Meds/allergies: ibuprofen only, no allergies;  
6) Social/exposures: adolescent, baseball player, no toxins;  
7) Physical exam: focal mid-femoral tenderness, no swelling, normal pulses/muscle bulk (inferred as not reported abnormal);  
8) Diagnostics: X-ray (lytic + periosteal reaction), CBC (leukocytosis), ESR (elevated), biopsy (small round blue cells).  
No further diagnostic information can be obtained within turn limit. Ready to [FINISH].

[FINISH]

